# THE BOARD'S OTHER NAME, SERVING THE BOARD — not redirecting to it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS EXISTS INSTEAD OF oldboard.caddy'S REDIRECT
#
# Moving the board from liuxuesheng.io to thexchange.app by pointing the old
# name at the new one looks free and is not. It signs everybody out.
#
# `board_in` and `board_wait` are set with no Domain attribute, so they are
# HOST-ONLY cookies — they belong to liuxuesheng.io and are not sent to
# thexchange.app, whatever redirect got the browser there. `board:device` in
# localStorage is per-origin and goes the same way. So a 301 delivers every
# member to a door asking for an invitation, and every person on the waiting
# list to a form that makes a SECOND row with an empty card on it. That last
# one is exactly what the wait cookie was built to prevent — the note above
# waitCookie in server.js is about this failure, in the smaller case of Safari
# clearing storage after seven days.
#
# Five members and forty-seven people in the queue is not a number that
# survives being asked to start again.
#
# SO BOTH NAMES SERVE THE BOARD. Nobody is signed out, because nobody moves:
# a member who has been using liuxuesheng.io keeps using liuxuesheng.io and
# keeps their cookie, while the app, new invites and anybody arriving fresh
# are on thexchange.app. The board itself does not care — it builds every
# absolute address it prints from the Host header of the request (see the
# three `const origin =` lines in server.js), so a link made on one name stays
# on that name.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, AND IT IS WORTH SAYING PLAINLY: two origins is two cookie
# jars. Somebody who signs in on one name is a stranger on the other, once,
# and has to get in again — by the six-digit code in /api/signin if they left
# an address, or a fresh key from the panel if they did not. That is a cost
# paid by whoever chooses to move, at a moment they chose, instead of being
# paid by all fifty-two people at once on the evening of a deploy.
#
# WHEN THE OLD NAME IS RETIRED, which is once nobody is arriving on it any
# more: empty this variable and set TOMSCODING_BOARD_OLD_DOMAIN to the same
# host instead. oldboard.caddy then 301s it, and by then a redirect costs
# nothing because there is no session left on it to lose.
#
# NEVER BOTH AT ONCE. Two site blocks sharing an address is a startup failure
# that takes every other site on this box down with it — `make caddy-check`
# catches it before a reload can, and is the reason that target exists.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE BLOCK IS A COPY OF board.caddy's, DELIBERATELY.
#
# Caddy can factor this into a snippet, and a snippet is the right tool. It is
# also syntax this config has never used, on a file where a mistake does not
# degrade anything — it stops Caddy, and every site on the box with it. This
# is the same shape as the block one file over, which is running.
#
# So: a copy, and the two stay in step by hand. Change one, change the other.
{$TOMSCODING_BOARD_ALSO:boardalso.localhost} {
	encode zstd gzip

	header {
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		-Server
	}

	@admin path /admin /admin/
	redir @admin https://{$TOMSCODING_FEED_DOMAIN}/

	@count path /a/e /a/lx.js
	handle @count {
		uri strip_prefix /a
		reverse_proxy analytics:3000
	}

	handle {
		reverse_proxy board:8080
	}
}
